Experiment 6: Champagne Tower
Aim

To find how full a particular glass is in a champagne tower after pouring a given amount of champagne into the top glass.

Algorithm
Make a table tower[row][glass] and put all the poured champagne in tower[0][0].
Go through the rows from 0 to query_row.
For each glass, if it holds more than 1 cup, the extra amount (tower[r][c] - 1) / 2 flows into each of the two glasses below it: tower[r+1][c] and tower[r+1][c+1].
After the last row, the answer is the amount in the queried glass, with a maximum of 1 because a glass cannot hold more than 1 cup.
Pseudo code
ChampagneTower(poured, query_row, query_glass)
  tower[0][0] = poured
  for r = 0 to query_row
      for c = 0 to r
          if tower[r][c] > 1
              extra = (tower[r][c] - 1) / 2
              tower[r+1][c]   = tower[r+1][c]   + extra
              tower[r+1][c+1] = tower[r+1][c+1] + extra
  answer = min(1, tower[query_row][query_glass])
  print answer
Source code (C)
c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int poured = 4, query_row = 2, query_glass = 1;
    double tower[102][102] = {0};
    double extra, answer;
    int r, c;

    tower[0][0] = poured;

    for (r = 0; r <= query_row; r++) {
        for (c = 0; c <= r; c++) {
            if (tower[r][c] > 1) {
                extra = (tower[r][c] - 1) / 2;
                tower[r + 1][c] += extra;
                tower[r + 1][c + 1] += extra;
            }
        }
    }

    answer = tower[query_row][query_glass];
    if (answer > 1)
        answer = 1;

    printf("Glass (%d, %d) is %.2f full\n", query_row, query_glass, answer);
    return 0;
}
Output
Glass (2, 1) is 0.50 full
Result

After pouring 4 cups into the top glass, the glass at row 2, position 1 is 0.50 full.

Time complexity: O(query_row²)
Space complexity: O(100 × 100), which is a fixed-size table.
